// All examples verified on morloc 0.112.0 (dev, 2026-11-18): the
// three-language `foo` example (`./recs foo Bob 40` gives age 43), the
// `people` dataclass, Rust and recursive-record examples, the kind error,
// the legacy warning and the `morloc types` file lists (C++, Rust). The
// record-literal error messages were verified on 0.100.2 and are unchanged.

[[records]]
=== Records

A record is a named, fixed set of named fields. It is the right shape when a
tuple would leave you counting positions.

[source, morloc]
----
record Person = Person
    { name :: Str
    , age :: Int
    }
----

The same declaration has a layout form, one field per indented line, which
reads better for long records:

[source, morloc]
----
record Person where
  name :: Str
  age :: Int
----

That declaration is all a record needs. Every language gets a native form of it
without your saying anything: a `dict` in Python, a named `list` in R, and a
`struct` the compiler writes for you in {cpp} and Rust. Internally the layout is
positional, but the surface language always binds by name.

==== Native representations

The forms Morloc gives a record, and how code you source names it:

[cols="1,2,2", options="header"]
|===
| Language | The record is | A sourced function names it as
| Python | a `dict` keyed by field name | a `dict`; nothing to import
| R | a named `list` | a `list`; nothing to import
| {cpp} | a generated `struct` with one member per field | `mlc::types::_local::recs::Person`, from `#include "mlc/types/_local/recs.hpp"`
| Rust | a generated `struct` with one `pub` field per field | `crate::mlc::types::_local::recs::Person`
|===

The generated name follows the module that declares the record. `recs` is that
module here, and `_local` marks it as one of your program's own modules; a
module of an installed package sits under its package's name instead. The
compiler writes the {cpp} header and the Rust module before it compiles your
sources, so a source can include or `use` them like any other file.

[source, cpp]
.foo.hpp
----
#pragma once
#include "mlc/types/_local/recs.hpp"

using mlc::types::_local::recs::Person;

Person incAge(Person person){
    person.age++;
    return person;
}
----

[source, rust]
.foo.rs
----
use crate::mlc::types::_local::recs::Person;

pub fn inc_age(person: &Person) -> Person {
    Person { name: person.name.clone(), age: person.age + 1 }
}
----

The R and Python sides operate on their native containers directly. Each
returns a changed copy rather than changing its argument; the {cpp} version gets
its copy by taking the struct by value (<<sourced-values>>):

[source, r]
.foo.R
----
incAge <- function(person){
    person$age <- person$age + 1
    person
}
----

[source, python]
.foo.py
----
def incAge(person):
    return {**person, "age": person["age"] + 1}
----

===== Python classes

In Python a record can instead be an instance of a class the compiler writes.
You choose this with a *kind*, a bare word after the `+=>+`:

[source, morloc]
.people.loc
----
module people (older)

import root-py

record Person where
  name :: Str
  age :: Int

record Py => Person = dataclass

source Py from "older.py" ("incAge" as older)
older :: Person -> Person
----

`dataclass` gives a frozen, slotted dataclass, and `pydantic` a frozen pydantic
model; `dict` is the default. Code written for the `dict` form keeps working,
because a field can be read by subscript as well as by attribute. Since the
instance is frozen, return a changed copy:

[source, python]
.older.py
----
import dataclasses

def incAge(person):
    print(type(person).__qualname__, person.name, person["age"])
    return dataclasses.replace(person, age=person.age + 1)
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./people older '{"name":"Bob","age":40}'
Person Bob 40
{"name":"Bob","age":41}
----

A source that needs the class itself imports it from the generated package,
here `from mlc_types._local.people import Person`. Only the package that declares
a record may give it a class kind. A word the language does not define is an
error that lists the ones it does:

[source, console]
----
bad.loc:9:1: py has no record kind `pydantik`; its kinds are dataclass, dict, pydantic (a string names a type you write instead)
    |
  9 | record Py => Person = pydantik
    | ^
----

===== Writing the type yourself

A quoted string after the `+=>+` names a type you write instead of the
generated one. It is how you hand a record to an existing {cpp} or Rust type:

[source, morloc]
----
record Cpp => Person = "person_t"
----

Field names and types still come from the general declaration, so they are not
repeated. Your type must hold exactly the record's fields, under the same
names, because Morloc builds a value of it from those fields and nothing else.
A type parameter that no field uses, a phantom like the `k` of
`record Loc k where pos :: Int`, is best not passed to your type at all
(`record Rust => Loc k = "LocT"`): Rust refuses a struct that does not use its
parameter, and a marker field would be a field the record lacks.

Python has no such form yet. A record mapped to a Python class name you wrote
is still passed as a `dict`; classes you write yourself are a planned feature.

The strings `"dict"`, `"list"`, `"struct"` and `"OrderedDict"` are older
spellings of the default kinds. They still work, with a warning:

[source, console]
----
warning: legacy.loc:9:1: "dict" is the old spelling of the py record kind `dict`; write `= dict` instead
----

===== Recursive records

A record whose fields refer to the record itself needs no special treatment in
Python or R. In {cpp} and Rust the generated type is a *handle*: a small value
that shares an immutable struct of the fields, so that copying a tree copies
one pointer. Read a field through `->` in {cpp}; Rust's handle dereferences to
the fields:

[source, morloc]
----
record Tree where
  label :: Str
  kids :: [Tree]
----

[source, cpp]
----
inline int count(const Tree& t) {
    int n = 1;
    for (const auto& k : t->kids) n += count(k);
    return n;
}
----

[source, rust]
----
pub fn count_rs(t: &Tree) -> i64 {
    1 + t.kids.iter().map(count_rs).sum::<i64>()
}
----

To build one, give the handle a struct of its fields: `Tree(Tree_fields{...})`
in {cpp}, `Tree::new(TreeFields { ... })` in Rust.

==== Record literals match by field name

Field values bind to declared fields by *name*. The order in a literal is
irrelevant, so these two are the same value:

[source, morloc]
----
alice :: Person
alice = { name = "Alice", age = 30 }

alice2 :: Person
alice2 = { age = 30, name = "Alice" }
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./recs alice
{"name":"Alice","age":30}
$ ./recs alice2
{"name":"Alice","age":30}
----

A literal must mention every declared field exactly once. All three ways to get
that wrong are compile-time errors. The examples below all come from a
`recbad.loc` whose record is declared on one line:

[source, morloc]
----
record Person = Person { name :: Str, age :: Int }
----

Missing a field:

[source, console]
----
recbad.loc:8:8-25: error:
Record literal does not match declared type Person:
  missing field(s): age
  |
8 | bad1 = { name = "Alice" }
  |        ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^
----

Naming a field the record does not have:

[source, console]
----
recbad.loc:8:8-48: error:
Record literal does not match declared type Person:
  unknown field(s): weight
  |
8 | bad1 = { name = "Alice", age = 30, weight = 65 }
  |        ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^
----

Repeating a field:

[source, console]
----
recbad.loc:8:33: duplicate field in record literal: name
    |
  8 | bad1 = { name = "Alice", name = "Bob", age = 30 }
    |                                 ^
----

==== One record across three languages

Because the record has a native form in each language, a function that operates
on it can be sourced from any of them, and they compose:

[source, morloc]
.recs.loc
----
module recs (foo, alice)

import root-r
import root-py
import root-cpp

record Person where
  name :: Str
  age :: Int

source R from "foo.R" ("incAge" as rinc)
source Py from "foo.py" ("incAge" as pinc)
source Cpp from "foo.hpp" ("incAge" as cinc)

rinc :: Person -> Person
pinc :: Person -> Person
cinc :: Person -> Person

foo :: Str -> Int -> Person
foo name age
    = (rinc . pinc . cinc)
      { name = name, age = age }

alice :: Person
alice = { name = "Alice", age = 30 }
----

`foo` builds a `Person` and then increments its age three times, once in each
language, passing the record across two process boundaries on the way. The
sources are the `foo.R`, the first `foo.py` and the `foo.hpp` shown above:

[source, console]
----
$ ./recs foo Bob 40
{"name":"Bob","age":43}
----

Nothing in `foo.R`, `foo.py`, or `foo.hpp` knows the others exist. Each sees
only its own language's ordinary data structure.

==== Using the generated types outside Morloc

`morloc types` writes the generated files for a program to a directory of your
choice, so that a program that is not built by Morloc can use the same types:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc types recs.loc -o gen
$ find gen -type f | sort
gen/cpp/mlc/types/_local/recs.hpp
gen/py/mlc_types/__init__.py
gen/py/mlc_types/_builtin/__init__.py
----

There is one directory per language the program uses. For Rust it writes a
crate, with a `Cargo.toml`, that a Rust project can depend on.

A field may hold a type from a library your module depends on. The generated
file then has to include or import that library, so the dependency's entry in
`package.yaml` says where its types live: the `namespace` its names start with
and, in {cpp}, the headers to include. A Rust crate's namespace defaults to
the crate's name, and the crate becomes a dependency of the written crate:

[source, yaml]
.package.yaml
----
cpp-deps:
  nlohmann_json:
    version: "*"
    source: conda
    namespace: nlohmann
    import: nlohmann/json.hpp
rust-deps:
  ndarray: "0.16"
----

